---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`Privacy Policy | ${SITE_TITLE}`}
  description="How leonlins.com and Avoid Boring People handle personal information."
>
  <article class="privacy-policy">
    <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="updated">Effective September 17, 2026</p>

    <p>
      This policy explains how Leon Lin operates leonlins.com and the Avoid
      Boring People newsletter. Questions can be sent to
      <a href="mailto:contact@leonlins.com">contact@leonlins.com</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Information collected</h2>
    <p>
      If you subscribe to the newsletter, the information involved is your email
      address, subscription and consent records, confirmation and unsubscribe
      records, and limited security information used to prevent abusive sign-up
      attempts. The website may also receive standard technical information from
      your browser and hosting providers, such as IP address, device/browser
      details, and pages requested.
    </p>

    <h2>How information is used</h2>
    <p>
      Information is used to operate the site and newsletter, confirm a
      subscription, deliver requested writing, honour unsubscribe requests,
      prevent abuse, and maintain the security and reliability of the service.
      Newsletter data is not sold.
    </p>

    <h2>Newsletter providers and legacy subscriptions</h2>
    <p>
      The current public newsletter sign-up form is operated by Substack. If you
      subscribe through it, Substack also processes your information under its
      own terms and privacy practices. The planned owned newsletter uses Neon
      for subscriber records and Amazon Web Services Simple Email Service (SES)
      for delivery and delivery-event processing. The site is hosted on Vercel.
      These providers process information only as needed to provide their
      services.
    </p>
    <p>
      If legacy Substack subscribers are migrated in the future, the retained
      information will be limited to the subscription, consent, and suppression
      history needed to provide the newsletter and prevent accidental
      resubscription of people who opted out.
    </p>
    <p>
      When sign-up on this site is enabled, the subscription record also keeps
      how you arrived: the campaign tags on the link you followed, the referring
      site's domain, and the page you landed on, taken from the first such visit
      your browser remembers. That record carries no IP address, sets no cookie
      of its own, and is never used for advertising or profiling; it exists so
      the author can tell which channel produced a subscription. The current
      public sign-up form still runs on Substack, which stores none of it.
    </p>

    <h2>Choices and retention</h2>
    <p>
      Every newsletter will include an unsubscribe link. You can also contact
      <a href="mailto:contact@leonlins.com">contact@leonlins.com</a> for help with
      your subscription or privacy questions. Suppression records may be retained
      after an unsubscribe request as needed to honour that request and avoid sending
      you future newsletter email by mistake.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you unsubscribe and later change your mind, submitting the sign-up form
      again starts a fresh confirmation: your subscription is restored only
      after you confirm the new email, and no one can re-add you by resubmitting
      the form on your behalf. An address that was suppressed after a hard
      bounce or a spam complaint is not restored this way, because that
      suppression protects deliverability for everyone on the list.
    </p>
    <h2>Article discussion</h2>
    <p>
      Article discussions are hosted on this site rather than by a third-party
      comment provider. Posting a comment stores the display name you type, the
      comment text, and the time it was posted. To let you edit or delete your
      own comment for 30 minutes after posting, the site stores an anonymous
      random token in your browser and keeps only a one-way hash of it; the
      token is not linked to your newsletter subscription, email address, or any
      account, and there are no accounts to sign in to.
    </p>
    <p>
      Comments are public and are shown in full to any reader of the article.
      Please do not post anything you want kept private; the article page also
      offers a direct email link for private conversations. The site does not
      store IP addresses with comments and does not build comment analytics or
      profiles.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cloudflare Turnstile is used to check that a comment is posted by a person
      rather than a script. Turnstile runs in the page and Cloudflare processes
      the request needed to perform that check under its own privacy policy. To
      limit abuse, comments from one browser are limited to about three in a
      ten-minute window.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can delete your own comment from the article page for 30 minutes after
      posting it, which removes the comment text. After that window, contact
      <a href="mailto:contact@leonlins.com">contact@leonlins.com</a> to ask for a
      comment to be removed.
    </p>

    <h2>Analytics and third-party content</h2>
    <p>
      The site may use a privacy-conscious Google Analytics configuration when
      enabled and reports lightweight engagement events such as a comment being
      posted; those events carry no comment text, name, or commenter identifier.
      Newsletter email carries no open or click tracking, so the author's
      newsletter reports show subscription counts and delivery outcomes rather
      than whether you opened or clicked anything. This site does not use
      behavioural advertising, profiling, or a third-party analytics product for
      the newsletter.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      Depending on where you live, privacy law may give you rights to request
      access, correction, deletion, restriction, or objection to certain uses of
      personal information. Contact
      <a href="mailto:contact@leonlins.com">contact@leonlins.com</a> to make a request.
      You may also have the right to complain to the privacy regulator where you
      live.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
      This policy may be updated when the site or newsletter changes. The
      effective date above will be updated when material changes are published.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .privacy-policy {
    max-width: 760px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .updated {
    color: rgb(var(--color-text-muted));
    margin-top: -1rem;
  }

  h2 {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }
</style>
